  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1141 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nOur area will remain on the western edge of an upper level ridge,\ncentered over the Lower Mississippi Valley region, on Wednesday. \nThe front is likely to lift back north during the day with \nsoutherly flow across the S Plains. High temperatures will remain \nnearly steady with another day of advisory-level heat indices \nexpected for much of the area. Additionally, higher PWATs will \nmigrate into E OK and W AR, resulting in a slightly higher \ncoverage of afternoon showers/thunderstorms during the afternoon \nand early evening. Still, rainfall will be spotty at best with dry\nconditions remaining the rule.\n\nThe ridge drifts westward during late week, becoming centered \nnearly overhead Thursday into Friday. This will result in \ncontinued warm and predominantly dry conditions for the area. \nAnother cold front will be pushed south toward the forecast area \nthis weekend as a stronger upper trough moves through the \nCentral/Northern Plains. With upper level support closer, this \nfront is currently projected to push into our area, if not clear \nit, early next week. Higher rain and thunder chances develop \nduring this time (Sat-Mon), but will be dependent on the position \nof the front. Temperatures should cool off quite a bit going into \nearly next week, with many locations potentially falling back to \nnear or slightly below average.",
